+# Cancellation
+
+For whoever changes cancellation or answers a member who cannot cancel a pantry order. The cutoff keeps app
+cancellations ahead of packing: late cancellations left packed bags on the shelf and fresh food went to waste.
+
+## Cutoff
+
+A member can cancel only before the instant two hours before their pickup slot starts. At that instant and later,
+cancellation is refused. For a 17:00 pickup, cancellation is allowed before 15:00 and refused at 15:00 or later.
+The eligibility check is [`can_cancel(pickup_starts, now)`](../../larder/cancellation.py); it returns a boolean.
+
+## Why packing sets the boundary
+
+The pantry lead reported three consecutive Saturdays with packed bags left behind, including nine bags containing
+milk, bread and greens after members cancelled at 16:50 for a 17:00 pickup. A banner asking members to cancel earlier
+had been present since summer, but 25 bags of fresh food had already been thrown away in October. Keeping the banner
+alone had not prevented the waste.
+
+The packing shift starts two hours before each slot. The operations volunteer reported that cancellations before
+packing cost nothing, while cancellations after packing cost a bag. Refusing app cancellations from that boundary
+was agreed in [issue 31](../../notes/issue-31.md), which also records the maintainer's shipment confirmation.
+
+## After the cutoff
+
+The agreed fallback is for a member who cannot come to call the pantry; volunteers decide what to do. The pantry
+lead required the pantry's phone number in the refusal message. The repository contains only the eligibility check:
+it supplies neither a refusal message nor a phone number, so it does not establish whether that message is present
+in the app. The contact number must come from the pantry.
